# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
boethius-1.md:1: argue/readings: S5 is read 2 ways: boethius-1.logic.r2 and boethius-1.logic.r2.2 have nothing from it; boethius-1.logic.r2.3 has "beautiful → ¬concerns_thee"; they differ when only "these things are beautiful in their own nature" and "their beauty is anything to thee" are true, which the first allows
boethius-1.md:1: argue/answered: S5: answered no, which keeps boethius-1.logic.r2.3, to: Suppose "these things are beautiful in their own nature" and "their beauty is anything to thee" are true. Is S5, "While if they are beautiful in their own nature, what is that to thee?", true then?
boethius-1.md:1: argue/readings: S6 is read 2 ways: boethius-1.logic.r2 and boethius-1.logic.r2.3 have "pleasing_without"; boethius-1.logic.r2.2 has nothing from it; they differ when nothing is true, which the second allows
boethius-1.md:1: argue/answered: S6: answered no, which keeps boethius-1.logic.r2 and boethius-1.logic.r2.3, to: Suppose "they would have been no less pleasing in themselves though never among thy possessions" is false. Is S6, "They would have been not less pleasing in themselves, though never included among thy possessions.", true then?
boethius-1.logic.r2: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 3 ways: boethius-1.logic.r2 has "servants_vicious ∨ servants_honest" and "servants_burden ∨ virtue_others → ¬things_thine" and "pleasing_without ∧ ¬precious_from_riches → ¬concerns_thee" and "¬things_thine ∧ ¬concerns_thee → ¬grieve_justified"; boethius-1.logic.r2.2 has "servants_vicious ∨ servants_honest" and "servants_burden ∨ virtue_others → ¬things_thine" and "¬precious_from_riches → pleasing_without" and "pleasing_without → ¬concerns_thee" and "¬concerns_thee → ¬grieve_justified"; they differ when only "the servants behave viciously", "the servants are honest", "the servants are a ruinous burden to thy house and dangerous to their master", "the servants' good is other men's virtue, which cannot be counted among thy possessions", "their beauty is anything to thee", "they would have been no less pleasing in themselves though never among thy possessions" and "they derive their preciousness from being counted in thy riches" are true, which the first allows; boethius-1.logic.r2.3 has "servants_vicious ∨ servants_honest" and "servants_burden ∨ virtue_others → ¬things_thine"
boethius-1.md:1: argue/readings: the conclusion is read 2 ways: boethius-1.logic.r2 and boethius-1.logic.r2.2 have "¬grieve_justified"; boethius-1.logic.r2.3 has "¬things_thine"; they differ when only "the things thou reckonest among thy possessions are really thine" is true, which the first allows
boethius-1.logic.r2: argue/readings: the main conclusion is read at 2 sentences: boethius-1.logic.r2 and boethius-1.logic.r2.2 put it at S4, "And if there is in them no beauty to be desired, why shouldst thou either grieve for their loss or find joy in their continued possession?"; boethius-1.logic.r2.3 at S3, "From all which 'tis plainly proved that not one of these things which thou reckonest in the number of thy possessions is really thine."
boethius-1.logic.r2: argue/answered: M: answered no, which keeps boethius-1.logic.r2.3, to: Is the text's main conclusion S4, "And if there is in them no beauty to be desired, why shouldst thou either grieve for their loss or find joy in their continued possession?", rather than S3, "From all which 'tis plainly proved that not one of these things which thou reckonest in the number of thy possessions is really thine."?
boethius-1.logic.r2: argue/readings: U1: boethius-1.logic.r2 and boethius-1.logic.r2.2 take for granted "if (they would have been no less pleasing in themselves though never among thy possessions and it is not so that they derive their preciousness from being counted in thy riches), then it is not so that their beauty is anything to thee"; boethius-1.logic.r2.3 does not
boethius-1.logic.r2: argue/answered: U1: answered yes, which keeps boethius-1.logic.r2 and boethius-1.logic.r2.2, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "if (they would have been no less pleasing in themselves though never among thy possessions and it is not so that they derive their preciousness from being counted in thy riches), then it is not so that their beauty is anything to thee"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
boethius-1.logic.r2: argue/readings: U2: boethius-1.logic.r2 and boethius-1.logic.r2.2 take for granted "if (it is not so that the things thou reckonest among thy possessions are really thine and it is not so that their beauty is anything to thee), then it is not so that thou hast reason to grieve for their loss or find joy in their continued possession"; boethius-1.logic.r2.3 does not
boethius-1.logic.r2: argue/answered: U2: answered yes, which keeps boethius-1.logic.r2 and boethius-1.logic.r2.2, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "if (it is not so that the things thou reckonest among thy possessions are really thine and it is not so that their beauty is anything to thee), then it is not so that thou hast reason to grieve for their loss or find joy in their continued possession"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
boethius-1.logic.r2: argue/readings: U3: boethius-1.logic.r2 and boethius-1.logic.r2.2 take for granted "if they would have been no less pleasing in themselves though never among thy possessions, then it is not so that their beauty is anything to thee"; boethius-1.logic.r2.3 does not
boethius-1.logic.r2: argue/answered: U3: answered yes, which keeps boethius-1.logic.r2 and boethius-1.logic.r2.2, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "if they would have been no less pleasing in themselves though never among thy possessions, then it is not so that their beauty is anything to thee"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
boethius-1.logic.r2: argue/readings: U4: boethius-1.logic.r2 and boethius-1.logic.r2.2 take for granted "if it is not so that their beauty is anything to thee, then it is not so that thou hast reason to grieve for their loss or find joy in their continued possession"; boethius-1.logic.r2.3 does not
boethius-1.logic.r2: argue/answered: U4: answered yes, which keeps boethius-1.logic.r2 and boethius-1.logic.r2.2, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "if it is not so that their beauty is anything to thee, then it is not so that thou hast reason to grieve for their loss or find joy in their continued possession"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
